course: dictionary
lesson: 753
title: Context clues: bauble
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Read the words around the new word first. «The first Christmas without Da, Cian's family hangs Da's favourite bauble, a wonky robin, right at the front of the tree» gives you clues: the family hangs the bauble, and it goes at the front of the tree.
explain: So a bauble here is a decoration for the tree. Ask yourself what word would fit: "decoration" fits, so try it in the sentence. The sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: The sentence also shows feeling. The bauble is special because it was Da's favourite, and the family tells the story of how he fell off the ladder. A bauble can be worth very little in money, but it can be worth a great deal to the people who love it.
fact: A bauble is a shiny ball or decoration hung on a Christmas tree.
fact: A bauble can also be a small, cheap piece of jewellery or a pretty object of little value.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, bauble most often means a Christmas tree decoration.
fact: Useful phrases include "Christmas baubles", "glass baubles" and "a mere bauble".
fact: "A mere bauble" can describe something cheap or of little value, and it can show that someone thinks it is worth nothing.
exercise: In «The first Christmas without Da, Cian's family hangs Da's favourite bauble, a wonky robin, right at the front of the tree», what is a bauble? | A kind of Christmas dinner | A bird that lives in the garden | *A decoration hung on the tree | A ladder used for climbing
exercise: In the same sentence, where does the family hang the bauble? | On the back of the tree | In the kitchen | Under the sofa | *At the front of the tree
exercise: A character says, "It's just a mere bauble." What does this suggest? | The object is very expensive and rare | The object is a type of food | *The object has little real value | The object is a large, heavy bell
test: Mia says a bauble is a type of cake. Is she right? | Yes, a bauble is a cake | *No, a bauble is a decoration, not a cake | Not sure, so guess | Yes, but only at Christmas
test: Which word in «The first Christmas without Da, Cian's family hangs Da's favourite bauble, a wonky robin, right at the front of the tree» is a clue that the bauble is hung up? | ladder | *hangs | favourite | robin
exam: Which meaning fits best when you swap it into «The first Christmas without Da, Cian's family hangs Da's favourite bauble, a wonky robin, right at the front of the tree»? | a pair of shoes | a school bag | *a decoration for the tree | a cake for the table
